                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'm deep into the board game hobby, and Flash Point is my favourite cooperative board game, so I was really looking forward to this release. Played 1.0.1.\n\nThis is my favourite video game based on a board game. Most are obvious mobile ports, that can be fine too and I've bought some since they're priced pretty cheap. The developers made a proper video game here, and it's priced appropriately -- and fairly, in my opinion. The game has a lot of polish and feels like a step up from other board game ports.\n\nIt's very true to the board game, with the different difficulty and complexity levels represented. It has a great variety of buildings, which is an improvement over the board game. As a fan of the board game, this is everything I was looking for. It's great for casual play, for players who like to think and puzzle out a strategy.\n\nSome of the other reviews I've read had complaints I'd like to address:\n[list]\n[*]No undo feature. It'd be nice, but misclicking in this game doesn't feel more punishing than misclicking in a more urgent setting (such as a real-time game). I haven't seen it as a huge problem.\n[*]Family version (simpler ruleset) is too hard. These reviews might have been written before 1.0.1, which introduced multiple difficulty levels. I can get perfect (rescue all 10 people) most of the time in Recruit (easy) and so far I've always won (7+ rescues) on Veteran (medium). So I don't see that difficulty, but I am familiar with the board game. If you rush in and don't plan ahead, you might curse bad luck, but planning ahead can often make bad luck less likely.\n[/list]\n\nThe tutorial isn't very good; it helps learn the rules of the game but doesn't help much learning the interface. You might have to experiment to learn.\n\nI'm no expert, but if you find the game difficult, here are some tips that might help.\n[list]\n[*][b]Play with 2-4 firefighters.[/b] This is probably the sweet spot. You want enough firefighters to have good coverage around the building, but too many and a lot can happen before a firefighter's turn comes around again. A rule of thumb, have a similar number of firefighters are there are outer doors.\n[*][b]Don't move unless you're able to do something at your destination. Otherwise, it's better to save actions.[/b] Just moving can be a waste, because when that firefighter's turn comes again, the board's changed, and maybe you'll want to change his destination. Move if you can do something there, like rescue or put out a fire. Otherwise, bank your actions so your next turn you can do more. Maybe a situation will develop, and you can then change your mind and go to the hotspot with lots of actions.\n[*][b]Don't end a firefighter's turn in a dangerous position[/b], that is, next to a fire. If you can't put it out that turn, better to do as above -- don't even move, and just save your actions to have a big next turn.\n[*][b]Conserve movement.[/b] Don't waste too many actions moving back and forth. If you're rescuing someone and some fires pop up nearby, go ahead and put out those fires on the way out. That's better than rescuing the person and coming back, wasting movement. Just remember not to end your turn in harm's way.\n[*][b]Prioritise saving people who have smoke in their space or fire in an adjacent space.[/b] In both cases, one spark can result in fire spreading to their space, killing them. Pull them out of immediate danger, or extinguish around them to buy them some time.\n[*][b]Prioritise putting out smoke/fire if there's more next to it.[/b] A big smoky area can light up full of fire by a single spark. A big firey area is more likely to cause explosions, resulting in more fire and building damage. Putting out the central smoke/fire can break it up and make those things less likely to happen. If there's a row of three spaces smokey or on fire, at least put out the middle one.\n[*][b]Remember, it's a strategy board game.[/b] There's no timer, so you can take your time to plan out your move. And all of the rules are clear and up-front, so the game is never unfair. You can have bad luck, but the more you play, the more you learn and understand the rules of play, the less bad luck you'll have.\n[/list]",
